WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.735
&gt;&gt; (narrator)
An inguinal hernia

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:04.704
is the abnormal protrusion
of abdominal contents,

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.072
such as the
intestine,

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:08.408
through an area of weakness
in the groin muscles.

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:10.844
The protruding intestine
resides in what is known

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.445
as a "hernia sac."

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:16.182
In some cases, untreated
hernias cause complications.

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:18.518
For example, if part
of the intestines

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:20.420
becomes stuck in
the hernia opening,

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.489
its blood supply
may be cut off,

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.991
leading to the death
of intestinal tissue.

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:27.560
This is called a
"strangulated hernia."

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.262
If a surgeon feels
you are at risk

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:32.599
for this complication, he or
she will likely recommend

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.567
elective
hernia repair.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:37.470
Traditional open
inguinal hernia repair

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:40.673
requires a relatively
large single incision.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.709
Newer laparoscopic
techniques

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:46.579
require only tiny keyhole
incisions, or puncture wounds,

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:50.183
which generally result in
a shorter recovery period.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.751
When you arrive
at the hospital,

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.686
an intravenous line
will be started

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.956
and you may be given a
sedative to help you relax.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:59.192
Laparoscopic inguinal
hernia repairs

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:01.194
are done under
general anesthesia,

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:04.197
which will put you to sleep for
the duration of the operation.

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.599
A breathing tube will
be temporarily inserted

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:08.668
through your mouth
and into your throat

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:10.703
to help you breathe
during the operation.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.173
A catheter may also be
placed in your bladder

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.474
to drain
your urine.

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:17.343
These will be removed at
the end of the procedure.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.780
An uncomplicated laparoscopic
inguinal hernia repair

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:23.783
usually takes between
30 and 60 minutes.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.187
Depending on your situation
and your surgeon's experience,

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.322
he or she will choose
one of two types

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:31.057
of laparoscopic
approaches--

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.593
extraperitoneal
laparoscopic repair

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.729
or trans-abdominal
preperitoneal repair.

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:38.465
To gain access
to the hernia,

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.935
your surgeon will use sharp
instruments called "trocars"

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.938
to create three small
holes or ports

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:46.573
through your
abdominal wall.

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:48.575
The location of these
ports will vary,

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.443
depending on your
surgeon's preference

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.178
and the type
of procedure.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.447
One of these ports
is usually located

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:56.950
at your navel
or umbilicus.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.352
Carbon dioxide gas
will then be pumped

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:02.322
through the umbilical port
to puff up your abdomen,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.225
so its contents can be
viewed more easily.

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:08.294
Next, your surgeon will
insert the laparoscope

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:10.096
through the
umbilical port.

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:13.500
Images from its camera are
projected onto a video monitor

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:15.034
in the
operating room.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.570
Your surgeon will then
pass various instruments

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.272
through the
other ports.

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:24.511
These will be used to
dissect away the hernia sac

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.847
and to tack a piece of mesh
over the hernia opening.

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:29.382
This will prevent
the intestine

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:32.752
from slipping back
through the opening.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.954
Before removing
the laparoscope,

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.257
your surgeon will take
one final look around

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:40.226
for areas of bleeding
or other damage.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.362
A port valve is
briefly left in place

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:45.999
to allow all the carbon dioxide
to escape from the abdomen.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.667
Finally, the
keyhole incisions

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:50.036
are closed with
sutures or staples,

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:51.938
and covered
with bandages.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:54.707
After the surgery, your
breathing tube and catheter

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:55.909
will be removed,

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:58.811
and you will be taken to the
recovery area for monitoring.

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.980
You'll be given pain
medication, as needed.

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:02.615
When you're able
to drink liquids,

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:05.151
your intravenous line
will be removed.

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:07.120
Most patients can
leave the hospital

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:10.223
three to five hours
after the procedure.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.826
(music)

